{
  "dom_id": "step-q107103",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Consider the vector space ${R}_{2}[t]$ of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $2,$ and its subset $A$ containing all polynomials of the form $at-b,$ where $a,b\\in R.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is closed under polynomial addition\n\nII. $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication\n\nIII. $A$ is a subspace of ${R}_{2}[t]$\n\nIV. $A$ is **not** a subspace of ${R}_{2}[t]$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that a subset of a vector space is a subspace if it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $f(t)={a}_{1}t-{b}_{1}$ and $g(t)={a}_{2}t-{b}_{2}$ are two polynomials from $A,$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)+g(t) & =({a}_{1}t-{b}_{1})+({a}_{2}t-{b}_{2}) \\\\  & =({a}_{1}+{a}_{2})t-({b}_{1}+{b}_{2}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under addition.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $f(t)=at-b\\in A$ and $k\\in R,$ then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}kf(t) & =k(at-b) \\\\  & =(ka)t-(kb),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a polynomial from $A.$ So, $A$ is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement III is true while statement IV is false. $A$ is a subspace because it's closed under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
